{"format":"edusim-world","version":1,"exportedAt":"2026-08-17T23:13:02.311Z","records":[{"id":"b86962da-2bf5-4c9e-a415-0669866805a3","kind":"world-theme","theme":"davinci","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[0,0,0],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]}},{"id":"6776af69-64cd-40e6-b7ea-a482364fb561","kind":"world-spawn","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[0,0,74],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]}},{"id":"921af95b-b136-4aad-9a29-ddd3e4d8c89d","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[0,0,-34],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"studio-building","options":{"width":36,"depth":26,"height":21,"seed":47}},{"id":"d9c43dfe-c239-494b-8690-e0f01ea91e14","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-26,0,20],"rotation":[0,0.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"ornithopter","options":{"span":34,"seed":7}},{"id":"9282d37c-29b1-4c4b-ab33-933552d29ba0","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-14,0,30],"rotation":[0,0.3080527810237764,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"It never flew","title":"The ornithopter","accent":"#8a6a42","body":"A flapping-wing glider, framed like a bat rather than feathered like a bird — Leonardo studied bats because a membrane wing is something a person could actually build. It could not have worked: no human has the power to flap a wing that size. He was wrong for the right reasons, and the wing shape is very nearly a modern hang glider."}},{"id":"6270b10e-1b9e-4f2c-838a-32f47a295a82","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[28,0,14],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"aerial-screw","options":{"height":19,"radius":9,"seed":13}},{"id":"b5aa343a-4ebe-4b68-94ff-38b83e8e741c","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[17,0,24],"rotation":[0,-0.3277385067805555,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"Also never flew","title":"The aerial screw","accent":"#b08d4a","body":"Often called the first helicopter. Four people walk the capstan to spin a linen screw. It could not have lifted: nothing stops the whole machine spinning the opposite way, and the crew would have to run faster than anyone can. A real helicopter needs a tail rotor for exactly the reason this one fails."}},{"id":"a91b0b83-4809-41b5-a113-2670b645830e","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-14,3.1493602205478225e-8,46],"rotation":[0,-0.6,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"vinci-cart","options":{"seed":19}},{"id":"c0e592e9-7de9-4c85-95be-752785478a5b","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-4,0.0007072069159094901,50],"rotation":[0,0.16514867741462683,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"This one works","title":"The self-propelled cart","accent":"#9a7a3c","body":"Driven by two coiled springs, and steered by pegs set between the gear teeth — change the pegs and it drives a different path. That makes it programmable, which is why it is often called the first robot. A working replica was built in 2004 and it drove exactly as drawn."}},{"id":"4cb177f1-7a7e-4299-8a9d-3a240d12a2f5","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[34,0.003278789834869742,44],"rotation":[0,-1.1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"war-machine","options":{"seed":41}},{"id":"1efddb1e-10ef-46d8-a157-6ae43bec64e6","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[24,0.0049735836946494605,48],"rotation":[0,-0.7454194762741583,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"Who paid for all this","title":"The war machines","accent":"#6b5031","body":"Leonardo wrote to the Duke of Milan offering himself as a military engineer, and listed painting last. The notebooks hold far more pages of catapults, bridges and cannon than of flying. Renaissance genius was funded by somebody, and it is worth knowing by whom."}},{"id":"b7fae8b7-fc69-4649-9eb9-839b31430be4","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-34,0,-4],"rotation":[0,0.4110645473898644,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"vitruvian-panel","options":{"size":9,"seed":23}},{"id":"209e2354-d12c-4c1d-9a45-6f2fd67f1e76","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-25,0,6],"rotation":[0,0.3523087246741417,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"c. 1490","title":"Vitruvian Man","accent":"#8a6a42","body":"A drawing that is really an argument: a human body fits both a circle and a square, but not from the same centre. The square is centred on the body; the circle is centred on the navel. Leonardo solved a problem the Roman architect Vitruvius had posed fifteen centuries earlier."}},{"id":"0a69c2fc-1afb-4194-a459-6bd8227c48d3","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[32,0,-6],"rotation":[0,-0.3805063771123649,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"easel-painting","options":{"width":5,"height":7,"seed":37}},{"id":"97d222ad-86a2-47d0-bb08-419836f2c23d","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[24,0,4],"rotation":[0,-0.3302973548292537,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"Unfinished, like most of them","title":"On the easel","accent":"#7a6448","body":"Leonardo finished perhaps fifteen paintings in forty years. He left work half-done constantly, went back to the Mona Lisa for sixteen years, and never delivered it. What is on this easel is a blocked-in underpainting with the drawing still showing — which is what most of his panels actually looked like."}},{"id":"656455ef-5298-4fe7-bc66-c0ca262fac3e","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-8,0,-12],"rotation":[0,0.2,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"workbench","options":{"length":11,"seed":29}},{"id":"564ae636-ee1d-4833-a4f9-c7b66d351f4e","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[10,0,-14],"rotation":[0,-0.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"workbench","options":{"length":9,"seed":31}},{"id":"3393d7c7-3721-4fc5-947a-6f605103518e","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-2,0,4],"rotation":[0,0.028563657838759995,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"codex-stand","options":{"seed":31,"sketch":"gears"}},{"id":"57372998-7349-448c-afd1-6c67cc1d15a9","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[12,0,12],"rotation":[0,-0.19118445959701444,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"codex-stand","options":{"seed":35,"sketch":"wing"}},{"id":"3672b581-95c2-49e2-bb55-751cec442c90","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-18,0,10],"rotation":[0,0.2741674511196588,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"codex-stand","options":{"seed":39,"sketch":"screw"}},{"id":"a1ef0a2a-9a41-450c-840e-878bd395072a","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[4,0,14],"rotation":[0,-0.06656816377582381,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"eyebrow":"Why it reads backwards","title":"Mirror writing","accent":"#6b5031","body":"Leonardo wrote right to left in mirror image through all 13,000 surviving pages. He was left-handed, and writing that way stops the ink smudging under your hand — the simplest explanation is usually the right one. It was not a code: anyone with a mirror can read it."}},{"id":"0de098d0-f4cb-456d-9e8d-6e073a4fcbb4","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-44,-0.013975799266417555,34],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"shade-tree","options":{"height":20,"seed":200,"leafColor":6060610}},{"id":"9c028ec0-a3a6-46a2-a26f-7449d76a9345","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[-48,-0.0008849641422979875,12],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"shade-tree","options":{"height":24,"seed":207,"leafColor":6060610}},{"id":"e7582b42-4609-449d-83cc-d50ccc85dc29","createdAt":1787008382311,"transform":{"position":[44,